When an enterprise starts building AI capabilities department by department, it accidentally recreates the worst integration anti-pattern of the 2000s: **point-to-point governance silos**.

HR builds a chatbot with custom Python wrappers around its leave endpoints. Finance builds a separate copilot with custom rules for invoice queries. IT Service Management wires an MCP server directly into an agent framework. Within six months, the organization has five different prompt-filtering schemes, five incompatible audit formats, and zero unified kill switches.

{% callout tone="insight" title="Architectural Principle: Application Independence" %}
AI governance should never be hardcoded inside individual business applications—and business-application logic should never be hardcoded inside the AI runtime. **Capabilities are governed data contracts, not source-code branches.**
{% /callout %}

{% video src="/videos/governed-autonomy-part-3.mp4" poster="/videos/governed-autonomy-part-3-poster.jpg" label="Watch · 1:30 · sound on" caption="The universal governance layer, from governance silos to one control plane." /%}

